---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import CoursePipeline from "../components/CoursePipeline.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A single gold brushstroke tapers to a point partway across a cream field; a thin grey dashed line continues where the stroke does not."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every week takes one discipline's way of leaving something out — a blank
    patch of silk, an unplayed beat, an argument that never states its
    conclusion — and asks what the omission was doing. You will read primary
    sources and make things: an ink wash with the brush lifted early, a scene
    cut two beats before the reveal, a proof that stops the instant it has
    enough. The seminar argues about whether each cut earned its keep. The
    three assessments ask you to keep a record of cuts you notice in the wild,
    make one deliberately in a medium of your choosing, and finally build an
    argument whose force depends on what you refuse to say.
  </p>

  <CoursePipeline />

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Built for a student who has already made something — a painting, a piece
    of writing, a design, a proof, a score — and is ready to be told that half
    of it should go. No single prerequisite discipline: what you need on day
    one is a portfolio of at least one finished thing, and the willingness to
    defend, out loud, why a particular part of it stays.
  </p>

  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card headingLevel="h3" title="The painter who keeps adding">
      <p>
        Every pass puts more in, because taking out feels like losing work.
        Week 3 hands you a tradition where the unpainted half is the subject.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card headingLevel="h3" title="The writer whose drafts only grow">
      <p>
        You can revise, but every revision is longer than the last. Week 5
        argues the iceberg theory with you, and against you.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card headingLevel="h3" title="The researcher with a null result">
      <p>
        It sits in a drawer because nobody publishes it. Week 9 treats that
        drawer as the course's hardest case, not its neatest one.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card headingLevel="h3" title="The designer told to simplify">
      <p>
        Nobody could say what it meant, so you deleted things until it looked
        quiet. Week 8 is about the difference between that and a decision.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>

  <p>
    None of those four is a prerequisite, and the course does not teach any of
    them a new technique. What they share is a finished thing and a reason to
    be suspicious of it — which is the only entry requirement that matters,
    because the semester works by argument rather than instruction. It is a
    poor fit for anyone who wants a method for making work shorter: the whole
    claim is that no such method exists, only cases, and the judgement you
    build by arguing about enough of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly discussion: what to bring, and what gets argued over.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The twelve cases the course moves through, week by week.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
